      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "f0b8b822-d8a2-519b-9994-281bfaded244",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: Commodore 16, Commodore 116, Prológica CP-400, Proof of Destruction\n- Companies: Commodore International, Prológica, Mastertronic\n- People: Shaun Southern\n- Systems: Commodore 64, 6502-compatible 7501 CPU, 6502-compatible 8501 CPU\n- Places: Europe, Brazil\n- Concepts: entry-level computer, fixed shooter\n\nFacts:\n- In 1984, Commodore International released the Commodore 16 as a home computer with a 6502-compatible 7501 or 8501 CPU.\n- In 1984, Commodore International intended the Commodore 16 to be an entry-level computer that would replace the VIC-20.\n- In 1984, Commodore International sold the cost-reduced Commodore 116 only in Europe.\n- In the middle of 1984, Prológica brought the CP-400 to the Brazilian market as a new machine in its CP personal computer series.\n- Prológica was a Brazilian company that made its own versions of 8-bit US computers.\n- In 1987, Shaun Southern wrote Proof of Destruction, a fixed shooter for the Commodore 64 and Commodore 16.\n- In 1987, Mastertronic published Proof of Destruction.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "caf5f178-f528-55c2-8d86-b2a124c64820",
        "text": "Page: Commodore 16. Sentence: The Commodore 16 is a home computer made by Commodore International with a 6502-compatible 7501 or 8501 CPU, released in 1984 and intended to be an entry-level computer to replace the VIC-20.\nPage: Commodore 16. Sentence: A cost-reduced version, the Commodore 116, was sold only in Europe.\nPage: Prológica CP-400. Sentence: In the middle of 1984 a Brazilian company called Prológica, which made its own versions of 8 bits US computers, brought to the Brazilian market a new equipment for its personal computer series called \"CP\" (shorten of Personal Computer in Portuguese).\nPage: Proof of Destruction. Sentence: Proof of Destruction (P.O.D.) is a 1987 fixed shooter for the Commodore 64 and Commodore 16 written by Shaun Southern and published by Mastertronic.",
